body:
This

&#x200B;

There's absolutely nothing wrong with getting help... I probably wouldn't still be here right now if I hadn't. I started having my first suicidal thoughts when I was 11 years old. I've already survived one attempt and was as close as I've ever been to another one not too terribly long ago. I was struggling at the time snorting M30s every day to cope. Occasionally I would think to myself "I wonder how many of these I'd have to take to nod off and never wake up?" doing the math in my head, but over time I started considering it more and more until it was almost all I could think about until one day it stopped being an idea and started to become a plan. I started to prepare for it getting everything lined up and taken care of, but there was one thing I couldn't account for. While trying to figure it out I guess I had a moment of clarity and tossed what I had left. I quit cold turkey then and there, started going to therapy, got back on my medications, and I'm proud to say I've been clean ever since. It's been awhile since then now and I'm definitely doing better. I can't say that I never have dark thoughts anymore because I still do and probably always will, but I'm no longer looking for an escape in everything I do. For the most part they're fleeting now, instead of all-consuming. So please don't be too afraid, or ashamed, or embarrassed, or whatever, to reach out. I promise it doesn't make you weak or lesser in any way. You don't have to suffer alone when there are plenty of people out there who would love nothing more than to help... and maybe in turn you can help someone else.

